{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01ShWMB5wXDbBmRpYV4jviS9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JvjHsdJiVaTL17RacnpjMS","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 reacts to R1's review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You have already submitted your review of the paper \"Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31% Temporal Instability in MMLU: A Psychometric Diagnostic at Scale.\" Now you are reading Reviewer 1's review and must provide your reactions — agreements, disagreements, and any points you want to revise in your own assessment.\n\n## YOUR ORIGINAL R2 REVIEW (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Summary**: Applies MH-DIF from educational testing to METABENCH. Well-executed application paper but novelty is \"known method, new domain.\" Related work gaps weaken positioning.\n\n**Your Scores**: Soundness 4, Excitement 2.5, Reproducibility 4, Overall 3.0, Confidence 4\n\n**Your Key Strengths**: (1) Thorough empirical controls, (2) Clear global-vs-differential distinction, (3) Zero FPR framework, (4) ~40 references, (5) Scale.\n\n**Your Key Weaknesses**: (1) Limited methodological novelty [Major], (2) Enrichment≈0.96 expected not surprising [Major], (3) Missing related work citations [Moderate], (4) \"Diagnostic lens\" retreat from stronger claims [Major], (5) GSM1K N=33 [Moderate], (6) Coarse temporal cohort [Moderate].\n\n---\n\n## R1 REVIEW (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Summary**: Core MH-DIF application is correct. Extensive confound controls. Semi-synthetic framework is well-designed. But 31% finding is descriptive without causal mechanism, pseudo R²=0.0007 needs scrutiny, GSM1K N=33 is underpowered, ConStat/DVD comparisons are structurally unfair, IRT model fit inadequately addressed, RL robustness concern.\n\n**R1 Scores**: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 3, Reproducibility 4, Overall 3.5, Confidence 4\n\n**R1 Key Strengths**: \n1. Correct and careful MH-DIF implementation (verified in code).\n2. Confound controls form coherent hierarchy: random (0.18%) → ability (2.9%) → temporal (31.3%) → within-family matched (28.3%). Nearest-neighbor matching convergence to ~28-29% is particularly telling.\n3. Semi-synthetic framework is most valuable methodological contribution. Cross-subject matching insight is algebraically guaranteed (ΔFPR=0.000 is structural, not sampling).\n4. Thorough threshold sensitivity (10.2%–48.9%).\n5. Strong reproducibility (public data, clear cohort definitions, code exists).\n6. Ground truth meta-critique is the most novel intellectual contribution.\n\n**R1 Key Weaknesses**:\n1. 31% instability is descriptive without causal mechanism — DIF detects \"that\" not \"why.\" The GSM1K negative correlation suggests DIF is NOT capturing contamination, but paper doesn't pursue what it IS capturing [Major].\n2. Pseudo R²=0.0007 is suspiciously low for logistic regression. Could indicate domain-level predictor too coarse (57 means → 12,508 items), model specification issues, or genuine independence. The 2.4pp figure's derivation not shown [Major].\n3. GSM1K N=33 with CI [-0.71, -0.16] should not be a numbered contribution [Major].\n4. ConStat/DVD comparisons structurally unfair: different item sets, model sets, granularities. Jaccard computed across incompatible sets [Moderate].\n5. 98% |Lz|>2 IRT misfit: total score is imperfect proxy for theta in multidimensional setting. Educational measurement specialists would use subject-specific scores [Moderate].\n6. RL robustness validation gap: N=13 reasoning-RL too small, paper claims complementarity to RL-fragile methods without demonstrating it [Moderate].\n\n**R1 Key Questions**:\n1. What is FPR of cross-subject matching on REAL (non-injected) temporal cohorts?\n2. Method says K = distinct total scores (thousands); code uses n_strata=5 (quantile binning). Which is used?\n3. Semi-synthetic items from ETS-A pool — systematically different from general population?\n4. Effective N after nearest-neighbor matching for Llama-2v3?\n5. BH FDR correction removes only 3 items — p-value distribution?\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nProvide your reactions to R1's review in this format:\n\n```\n### R2 Cross-Share Reactions\n\n**Agreements with R1** (points where R1 strengthens or validates your own assessment)\n\n**Disagreements with R1** (points where you think R1 is wrong or overly harsh/lenient)\n\n**Points I want to revise** (any changes to your own scores or assessment after reading R1)\n\n**Key unresolved questions for roundtable** (2-3 items that need discussion)\n```\n\nBe specific. Reference exact claims and scores.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3191,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7375,"cache_read_input_tokens":55711,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7375},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dfa3979-c7e7-46c7-acde-ccccfa135b4d","uuid":"a37bb80d-1d73-4fd6-b75d-5d6a4f165ba2"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"acf61b2b8028a9737","tool_use_id":"toolu_01JvjHsdJiVaTL17RacnpjMS","description":"R2 reacts to R1's review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You have already submitted your review of the paper \"Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31% Temporal Instability in MMLU: A Psychometric Diagnostic at Scale.\" Now you are reading Reviewer 1's review and must provide your reactions — agreements, disagreements, and any points you want to revise in your own assessment.\n\n## YOUR ORIGINAL R2 REVIEW (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Summary**: Applies MH-DIF from educational testing to METABENCH. Well-executed application paper but novelty is \"known method, new domain.\" Related work gaps weaken positioning.\n\n**Your Scores**: Soundness 4, Excitement 2.5, Reproducibility 4, Overall 3.0, Confidence 4\n\n**Your Key Strengths**: (1) Thorough empirical controls, (2) Clear global-vs-differential distinction, (3) Zero FPR framework, (4) ~40 references, (5) Scale.\n\n**Your Key Weaknesses**: (1) Limited methodological novelty [Major], (2) Enrichment≈0.96 expected not surprising [Major], (3) Missing related work citations [Moderate], (4) \"Diagnostic lens\" retreat from stronger claims [Major], (5) GSM1K N=33 [Moderate], (6) Coarse temporal cohort [Moderate].\n\n---\n\n## R1 REVIEW (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Summary**: Core MH-DIF application is correct. Extensive confound controls. Semi-synthetic framework is well-designed. But 31% finding is descriptive without causal mechanism, pseudo R²=0.0007 needs scrutiny, GSM1K N=33 is underpowered, ConStat/DVD comparisons are structurally unfair, IRT model fit inadequately addressed, RL robustness concern.\n\n**R1 Scores**: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 3, Reproducibility 4, Overall 3.5, Confidence 4\n\n**R1 Key Strengths**: \n1. Correct and careful MH-DIF implementation (verified in code).\n2. Confound controls form coherent hierarchy: random (0.18%) → ability (2.9%) → temporal (31.3%) → within-family matched (28.3%). Nearest-neighbor matching convergence to ~28-29% is particularly telling.\n3. Semi-synthetic framework is most valuable methodological contribution. Cross-subject matching insight is algebraically guaranteed (ΔFPR=0.000 is structural, not sampling).\n4. Thorough threshold sensitivity (10.2%–48.9%).\n5. Strong reproducibility (public data, clear cohort definitions, code exists).\n6. Ground truth meta-critique is the most novel intellectual contribution.\n\n**R1 Key Weaknesses**:\n1. 31% instability is descriptive without causal mechanism — DIF detects \"that\" not \"why.\" The GSM1K negative correlation suggests DIF is NOT capturing contamination, but paper doesn't pursue what it IS capturing [Major].\n2. Pseudo R²=0.0007 is suspiciously low for logistic regression. Could indicate domain-level predictor too coarse (57 means → 12,508 items), model specification issues, or genuine independence. The 2.4pp figure's derivation not shown [Major].\n3. GSM1K N=33 with CI [-0.71, -0.16] should not be a numbered contribution [Major].\n4. ConStat/DVD comparisons structurally unfair: different item sets, model sets, granularities. Jaccard computed across incompatible sets [Moderate].\n5. 98% |Lz|>2 IRT misfit: total score is imperfect proxy for theta in multidimensional setting. Educational measurement specialists would use subject-specific scores [Moderate].\n6. RL robustness validation gap: N=13 reasoning-RL too small, paper claims complementarity to RL-fragile methods without demonstrating it [Moderate].\n\n**R1 Key Questions**:\n1. What is FPR of cross-subject matching on REAL (non-injected) temporal cohorts?\n2. Method says K = distinct total scores (thousands); code uses n_strata=5 (quantile binning). Which is used?\n3. Semi-synthetic items from ETS-A pool — systematically different from general population?\n4. Effective N after nearest-neighbor matching for Llama-2v3?\n5. BH FDR correction removes only 3 items — p-value distribution?\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nProvide your reactions to R1's review in this format:\n\n```\n### R2 Cross-Share Reactions\n\n**Agreements with R1** (points where R1 strengthens or validates your own assessment)\n\n**Disagreements with R1** (points where you think R1 is wrong or overly harsh/lenient)\n\n**Points I want to revise** (any changes to your own scores or assessment after reading R1)\n\n**Key unresolved questions for roundtable** (2-3 items that need discussion)\n```\n\nBe specific. Reference exact claims and scores.\n","uuid":"8f32a12e-4cc5-4df4-90bc-980548954ae2","session_id":"2dfa3979-c7e7-46c7-acde-ccccfa135b4d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a roundtable discussion. You have already submitted your review of the paper \"Differential Item Functioning Reveals 31% Temporal Instability in MMLU: A Psychometric Diagnostic at Scale.\" Now you are reading Reviewer 1's review and must provide your reactions — agreements, disagreements, and any points you want to revise in your own assessment.\n\n## YOUR ORIGINAL R2 REVIEW (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Summary**: Applies MH-DIF from educational testing to METABENCH. Well-executed application paper but novelty is \"known method, new domain.\" Related work gaps weaken positioning.\n\n**Your Scores**: Soundness 4, Excitement 2.5, Reproducibility 4, Overall 3.0, Confidence 4\n\n**Your Key Strengths**: (1) Thorough empirical controls, (2) Clear global-vs-differential distinction, (3) Zero FPR framework, (4) ~40 references, (5) Scale.\n\n**Your Key Weaknesses**: (1) Limited methodological novelty [Major], (2) Enrichment≈0.96 expected not surprising [Major], (3) Missing related work citations [Moderate], (4) \"Diagnostic lens\" retreat from stronger claims [Major], (5) GSM1K N=33 [Moderate], (6) Coarse temporal cohort [Moderate].\n\n---\n\n## R1 REVIEW (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Summary**: Core MH-DIF application is correct. Extensive confound controls. Semi-synthetic framework is well-designed. But 31% finding is descriptive without causal mechanism, pseudo R²=0.0007 needs scrutiny, GSM1K N=33 is underpowered, ConStat/DVD comparisons are structurally unfair, IRT model fit inadequately addressed, RL robustness concern.\n\n**R1 Scores**: Soundness 3.5, Excitement 3, Reproducibility 4, Overall 3.5, Confidence 4\n\n**R1 Key Strengths**: \n1. Correct and careful MH-DIF implementation (verified in code).\n2. Confound controls form coherent hierarchy: random (0.18%) → ability (2.9%) → temporal (31.3%) → within-family matched (28.3%). Nearest-neighbor matching convergence to ~28-29% is particularly telling.\n3. Semi-synthetic framework is most valuable methodological contribution. Cross-subject matching insight is algebraically guaranteed (ΔFPR=0.000 is structural, not sampling).\n4. Thorough threshold sensitivity (10.2%–48.9%).\n5. Strong reproducibility (public data, clear cohort definitions, code exists).\n6. Ground truth meta-critique is the most novel intellectual contribution.\n\n**R1 Key Weaknesses**:\n1. 31% instability is descriptive without causal mechanism — DIF detects \"that\" not \"why.\" The GSM1K negative correlation suggests DIF is NOT capturing contamination, but paper doesn't pursue what it IS capturing [Major].\n2. Pseudo R²=0.0007 is suspiciously low for logistic regression. Could indicate domain-level predictor too coarse (57 means → 12,508 items), model specification issues, or genuine independence. The 2.4pp figure's derivation not shown [Major].\n3. GSM1K N=33 with CI [-0.71, -0.16] should not be a numbered contribution [Major].\n4. ConStat/DVD comparisons structurally unfair: different item sets, model sets, granularities. Jaccard computed across incompatible sets [Moderate].\n5. 98% |Lz|>2 IRT misfit: total score is imperfect proxy for theta in multidimensional setting. Educational measurement specialists would use subject-specific scores [Moderate].\n6. RL robustness validation gap: N=13 reasoning-RL too small, paper claims complementarity to RL-fragile methods without demonstrating it [Moderate].\n\n**R1 Key Questions**:\n1. What is FPR of cross-subject matching on REAL (non-injected) temporal cohorts?\n2. Method says K = distinct total scores (thousands); code uses n_strata=5 (quantile binning). Which is used?\n3. Semi-synthetic items from ETS-A pool — systematically different from general population?\n4. Effective N after nearest-neighbor matching for Llama-2v3?\n5. BH FDR correction removes only 3 items — p-value distribution?\n\n---\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nProvide your reactions to R1's review in this format:\n\n```\n### R2 Cross-Share Reactions\n\n**Agreements with R1** (points where R1 strengthens or validates your own assessment)\n\n**Disagreements with R1** (points where you think R1 is wrong or overly harsh/lenient)\n\n**Points I want to revise** (any changes to your own scores or assessment after reading R1)\n\n**Key unresolved questions for roundtable** (2-3 items that need discussion)\n```\n\nBe specific. Reference exact claims and scores.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01JvjHsdJiVaTL17RacnpjMS","session_id":"2dfa3979-c7e7-46c7-acde-ccccfa135b4d","uuid":"8fbeef67-f86d-4683-8401-8535452d4f9c","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:43:56.168Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R1SYbRtdC5Cp2ExokPj3P8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% ============================================================\n2\t% Benchmark Contamination Detection\n3\t% ============================================================\n4\t\n5\t@inproceedings{balloccu2024leak,\n6\t  title     = {Leak, Cheat, Repeat: Data Contamination and Evaluation Malpractices in Closed-Source {LLMs}},\n7\t  author    = {Balloccu, Simone and Schmidtova, Patrícia and Lango, Mateusz and Dusek, Ondrej},\n8\t  booktitle = {Proceedings of the 18th Conference of the European Chapter of the Association for Computational Linguistics (EACL)},\n9\t  year      = {2024},\n10\t  doi       = {10.18653/v1/2024.eacl-long.5},\n11\t  url       = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.03927}\n12\t}\n13\t\n14\t@inproceedings{dekoninck2024constat,\n15\t  title     = {{ConStat}: Performance-Based Contamination Detection in Large Language Models},\n16\t  author    = {Dekoninck, Jasper and Mueller, Mark Niklas and Vechev, Martin},\n17\t  booktitle = {Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS)},\n18\t  year      = {2024},\n19\t  url       = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.16281}\n20\t}\n21\t\n22\t@inproceedings{golchin2024timetravel,\n23\t  title     = {Time Travel in {LLMs}: Tracing Data Contamination in Large Language Models},\n24\t  author    = {Golchin, Shahriar and Surdeanu, Mihai},\n25\t  booktitle = {Proceedings of the International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR)},\n26\t  year      = {2024},\n27\t  url       = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08493}\n28\t}\n29\t\n30\t@inproceedings{li2024contamination,\n31\t  title     = {An Open-Source Data Contamination Report for Large Language Models},\n32\t  author    = {Li, Yucheng and Guo, Yunhao and Guerin, Frank and Lin, Chenghua},\n33\t  booktitle = {Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2024},\n34\t  year      = {2024},\n35\t  doi       = {10.18653/